¶
''Torsional Vibrations of Rods and Wires''.—The velocity of propagation of a torsional disturbance along a wire of circular section may be found by the transfer of momentum method, remembering that we must now replace linear momentum by angular momentum. Let the disturbance be supposed to travel unchanged in form from left to right with velocity <math>\text{U}</math>. Now suppose that the wire or rod is moved from right to left with velocity <math>\text{U}</math>. The disturbance is then fixed in space. Let <math>\text{A}</math> be a point in the disturbance and <math>\text{B}</math> a point in the undisturbed portion. The condition of the matter between <math>\text{A}</math> and <math>\text{B}</math> remains constant, though fresh matter keeps coming in at <math>\text{B}</math> and an equal quantity leaves at <math>\text{A}</math>. Hence the angular momentum of the part between <math>\text{A}</math> and <math>\text{B}</math> remains constant, or as much enters at <math>\text{B}</math> as leaves at <math>\text{A}</math>. But at <math>\text{B}</math> there is no torsion, and no torsion couple of one part of the wire on the next. So that no angular momentum enters at <math>\text{B}</math>, and therefore on the whole none leaves at <math>\text{A}</math>. The transfer of angular momentum through <math>\text{A}</math> is of two kinds—first, that due to the passage of rotating matter, and, secondly, that due to the couple with which matter to the right of <math>\text{A}</math> acts upon matter to the left of <math>\text{A}</math>. The mass of matter moving through <math>\text{A}</math> per second is <math>\rho\pi a^2\text{U}</math>, where <math>a</math> is the radius of the wire and <math>\rho</math> is its density. If <math>\theta</math> is the angle of twist, the angular velocity is <math>d\theta/dt</math>. The radius of gyration of the section is <math>\tfrac{1}{2}a^2</math>. Hence the angular momentum conveyed per second outwards is <math>\tfrac{1}{2}\rho\pi a^4\text{U}d\theta/dt</math>. The couple due to the twist of a wire of length <math>l</math> through <math>\phi</math> is <math>\text{G}=\tfrac{1}{2}n\pi a^4\phi/l</math>, and we may put <math>\phi/l=d\theta/dx</math>. Since no angular momentum goes out on the whole
{{block center|<math>\tfrac{1}{2}n\pi a^4d\theta/dx+\tfrac{1}{2}\rho\pi a^4\text{U}d\theta/dt=0</math>. (33)}}

But the condition of unchanged form requires that the matter shall twist through <math>(d\theta/dx)dx</math> while it is travelling over <math>dx</math>, ''i.e.'' in time <math>dx/\text{U}</math>.
{{block center|Then <math>\frac{d\theta}{dt}\,\frac{dx}{\text{U}}=-\frac{d\theta}{dx}dx</math> or <math>\frac{d\theta}{dt}=-\text{U}\frac{d\theta}{dx}</math>.}}
